==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=upstream}}

===Etymology===
{{prefix|en|up|stream}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ʌpˈstɹiːm/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-upstream.wav|Audio (southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːm|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|farther|further}}

# {{senseid|en|literally, of a flow of fluid}} In a [[direction]] [[against]] the [[flow]] of a [[current]] or [[stream]] of [[fluid]] (typically [[water]]); [[upriver]]. 
# {{senseid|en|figuratively, in general}} {{lb|en|metaphoric}} Occurring earlier than something else; {{lb|en|also, usually, especially}}<!--in human cognitive schemas, notwithstanding any {{w|post hoc ergo propter hoc|post hoc counterexamples}}--> being an influence on something else; causing a consequence for something else. 
#: {{ux|en|Input entry is '''upstream''' of input validation in the runtime process.}}
## {{senseid|en|figuratively, of oil moving through the oil industry}} {{lb|en|oil industry}} Involving [[exploration]] and [[pre-production]] rather than [[refining]] and [[selling]]. 
## {{senseid|en|figuratively, of computer networks}} {{lb|en|computer networking}} In the direction from the [[client]] to the [[server]]. 
## {{senseid|en|figuratively, of version control}} {{lb|en|open-source software}} Maintained, owned, or associated with the [[original]] [[developer]]s of the given [[software]]; in contrast to a modified version [[downstream]]. 
##* '''2013''', Matthew Helmke, ''Ubuntu Unleashed 2013 Edition: Covering 12.10 and 13.04'', Pearson Education
##*: You can also check the '''upstream''' and/or Debian bug trackers for open and closed bugs and the '''upstream''' revision history or newer release(s).
##* '''2012''', Jono Bacon, ''The Art of Community: Building the New Age of Participation'', O'Reilly Media, Inc., page 141
##*: If the developer knows the bug is an '''upstream''' bug but does not know which bug it is in the '''upstream''' bug tracker, he can add an '''''upstream''' task'' to the bug report.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Michael Dennis Scott|title=Scott on Outsourcing: Law and Practice|publisher=Aspen Publishers|page=198|passage=Because a user generally does not know the pedigree of the open source, it cannot know whether an '''upstream''' developer used intellectual property belonging to a third party in developing that software.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Tony Mancill|title=Linux Routers: A Primer for Network Administrators|publisher=Prentice Hall|page=190|passage=We'll walk through the steps to build FreeS/WAN using the '''upstream''' tarball.}}
## {{senseid|en|figuratively, of base pairs in nucleic acid sequences}} {{lb|en|biology}} Towards the [[leading]] [[end]] ([[5′&nbsp;end]]) of a [[DNA]] [[molecule]]. 

